1078 #edb/ref 17592186358745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360423 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee046ec2-08f8-5c58-bfb3-059a2ba841f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360507 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360507 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360507 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360507 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=3x-2y+z,$ where $x=s+t,$ $y={s}^{2}-{t}^{2}$ and $z=s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360507 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4381d9a9-c913-5a57-ad60-d264f0f8dbf1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360507 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2t)+(1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4t+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360553 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x=4t$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7bb11d2f-2294-5c9e-b733-36535d04471b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360553 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4+(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360557 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+y},$ $x={s}^{2}$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a57f510-aa58-5248-9a92-0fa5039eed64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360557 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{1+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{(1+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{1+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+s}\\cdot 2s-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2s}{1+s}-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s(1+s)-{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360568 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360568 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360568 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25aad332-4985-5005-bb5a-87ea911c6d16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360568 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186378033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378032 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8e3b0fb6-3358-5fd4-9918-187307cf0084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378033 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378033 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378033 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a08b852d-dd88-5d77-8ddb-331167273436" #ed