 #edb/ref 17592186437267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186437269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437271 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437271 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437271 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186437269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437271 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f729b4c9-529c-5fb2-a0e6-3a129de28dfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437272 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437272 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437272 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=x-y,$ where $x=-s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437272 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00d74228-1684-517b-8b3d-a3ad6a19bdf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437272 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437273 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437273 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437273 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437273 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab9cce6f-4b79-504d-bebd-6e1b2e2dc75d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186437273 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
